  "response": "The solar neutrino problem began in a South Dakota gold mine. Ray Davis led the Homestake experiment that first measured solar neutrinos, and only after that pioneering measurement was in hand did the experiment, even though the Homestake detector used gallium as its target material, record a striking result: it detected roughly one-third of the electron neutrinos predicted by theory. For decades the shortfall persisted across successive runs, and it raised a question that the Homestake data could not answer alone. A detector that counts only electron neutrinos cannot tell whether the missing particles were never produced in the quantity expected or were produced and then disappeared from view in transit.\n\nThe prediction against which Homestake was judged had its own pedigree. John Bahcall developed the standard solar model calculations of the expected neutrino flux, combining nuclear reaction rates, opacities, and stellar structure equations into a detailed account of the fusion processes in the solar core. Critics of the model argued that a small error in the core temperature would sharply reduce the boron-8 neutrino flux, to which the early detectors were most sensitive. That defence of the model's critics weakened over time, because helioseismology measurements of the Sun's interior agreed closely with the standard solar model. The oscillation frequencies of the solar surface trace the sound speed throughout the interior, and they matched the model's profile to within a fraction of a percent, leaving little room for the large adjustments a purely astrophysical explanation would require.\n\nThe decisive test required a detector that could count all neutrinos, not only the electron flavor. The Sudbury Neutrino Observatory used heavy water as its detection medium, whereas SNO's detector contained 1,000 tonnes of ordinary light water as its primary target. Deuterium made possible a measurement no earlier instrument could perform: SNO measured a neutral-current reaction that is equally sensitive to all three neutrino flavors, breaking deuterons apart regardless of whether the incoming neutrino was of electron, muon, or tau type. The total flux of boron-8 solar neutrinos measured by SNO agreed with the standard solar model prediction. In other words, SNO's count of boron-8 solar neutrinos of all flavors matched the number the standard solar model forecast. Set against the charged-current rate, which responds only to electron neutrinos and remained low, the agreement showed that the Sun was producing the expected neutrinos and that most of them arrived as some other flavor.\n\nThe SNO collaboration's 2002 results ultimately determined that the solar neutrino deficit did not stem from an error in the standard solar model, and with that the tension surrounding the Homestake shortfall was settled: Bahcall's calculation had been correct, and the missing third reflected the behavior of the particles rather than a flaw in solar physics. That determination supports the judgment that followed, for the Nobel Committee concluded that SNO's measurements showed solar neutrinos change identity on their way to Earth. The committee's conclusion led in turn to the finding that neutrinos have nonzero mass, which settles a question admitting only one answer: either that finding holds, or neutrinos are massless particles, and the two cannot both be true. Flavor change requires mass differences among the neutrino states, so the massless picture embedded in the original Standard Model could not survive.\n\nThe mechanism of the conversion still requires careful accounting. At least one of two processes is responsible, perhaps both: electron neutrinos from the Sun change flavor through vacuum oscillations during their journey to Earth, or electron neutrinos from the Sun change flavor through matter-enhanced MSW conversion inside the Sun. The relative contribution of each depends on neutrino energy, with the dense solar interior shaping the high-energy boron-8 neutrinos and vacuum effects governing the lower-energy part of the spectrum.",
